<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13466"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, May 05, 2005 - 7:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If he is not clinically insane but the defense tries to show that he is rendered temporily menatally incapcitated, disabled, impaired, incapable or defective by being under the influence of an alternative personality while commiting or communicating the acts of a serial killer then her testamony of him being excited about talking about BTK and leaving work to watch BTK coverage on TV kind of puts a whole in that approach.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
